{; WHAT ONE TRANSACTION IS, in Datahike's own vocabulary: a vector of
 ; operations, or its argument map carrying `:tx-data` and optional
 ; `:tx-meta`. Those two keys are unqualified because they are THEIRS
 ; (`reference-code/datahike/src/datahike/spec.cljc:66-67`), and
 ; renaming a dependency's keys to look like ours is the drift the
 ; grounding rule forbids.
 ; This form exists because the map arm was already on the live
 ; critical path while `transact!`'s contract said `[:vector :any]`,
 ; and Datahike's own spec admitted the map by accident — every map
 ; entry is a `coll?`. Declared, the mismatch is unrepresentable.
 ; Datahike database values are opaque runtime objects, but not anonymous:
 ; its maintained `datahike.db.utils/db?` predicate covers current, as-of,
 ; since, and history values. The generator constructs all four variants.
 :seon.db/database-value
 [:fn {:gen/gen seon.cluster.store/database-value-generator}
  seon.cluster.store/database-value?]
 ; Transaction operations are dependency-owned open data: entity maps or
 ; Datahike operation vectors. Their values are genuinely polymorphic at this
 ; boundary, so the one named `:seon.schema/value` exception is referenced
 ; instead of repeating anonymous `:any` slots throughout public contracts.
 :seon.store/transaction-operation
 [:or :map [:vector :seon.schema/value]]
 :seon.store/transaction-data
 [:vector :seon.store/transaction-operation]
 :seon.store/transaction
 [:or
  :seon.store/transaction-data
  [:map {:closed true}
   [:tx-data :seon.store/transaction-data]
   [:tx-meta {:optional true} :map]]]
 :seon.store/branch :keyword
 :seon.store/branch-connection [:fn {:gen/gen seon.cluster.store/connection-generator} seon.cluster.store/connection?]
 ; A connection OBJECT, live or released. The distinction is not
 ; pedantry: a started instance holds a connection that outlives its own
 ; liveness, and `stop!` is idempotent, so its second call necessarily
 ; receives a released one. Requiring liveness on the instance made the
 ; contract forbid the case stop! exists to handle.
 :seon.store/connection [:fn {:gen/gen seon.cluster.store/connection-generator} seon.cluster.store/connection-object?]
 :seon.store/dir [:string {:min 1}]
 :seon.store/lock-file [:string {:min 1}]
 :seon.store/store [:map {:closed true} [:seon.store/dir :seon.store/dir] [:seon.store/lock-file :seon.store/lock-file] [:seon.store/connection [:fn {:gen/gen seon.cluster.store/connection-generator} seon.cluster.store/connection?]] [:seon.store/lock [:fn {:gen/gen seon.cluster.store/file-lock-generator} seon.cluster.store/file-lock?]] [:seon.store/created? :boolean]]}
